GIBBS RING ARTIFACT (TRUNCATION ARTIFACT)

The Gibbs phenomenon arises from the mathematical properties of Fourier reconstruction. MRI acquires k-space data over a finite number of samples; when this truncated dataset is inverse-Fourier transformed, high-frequency components near sharp signal transitions are incompletely represented, producing a series of oscillating ringing bands that parallel high-contrast interfaces. The artifact is named for the mathematician J.W. Gibbs, who described this property of Fourier series in the context of signal discontinuities.
In breast MRI, Gibbs ringing is most visible at sharp interfaces with large signal differences: the skin-air boundary, the skin-parenchyma interface, and the margin of silicone implants. The rings appear as thin parallel bands of alternating bright and dark signal, closely spaced near the interface and diminishing in amplitude with distance. The artifact is more pronounced in the phase-encode direction when phase matrix is smaller than frequency matrix, and is generally more visible on high-contrast sequences such as T2-weighted imaging.
The artifact can be reduced by increasing the number of phase-encode steps to acquire more k-space data, at the cost of longer scan time. Zero-fill interpolation (filling k-space edges with zeros before reconstruction) increases apparent matrix size and smooths the ringing at the expense of true spatial resolution. K-space filtering techniques (such as Hamming or Hanning windowing) taper the k-space periphery, reducing ringing while introducing mild blurring. In clinical practice, recognition of the parallel-band pattern at expected high-contrast interfaces is usually sufficient to distinguish Gibbs artifact from true pathology.

