ENHANCEMENT ARTIFACTS: SUBTRACTION ERROR AND T1 SHINE-THROUGH

Dynamic contrast-enhanced breast MRI relies on digital subtraction of pre-contrast from post-contrast images to isolate enhancing tissue. Two distinct artifact mechanisms can produce false or misleading signal on subtraction images: subtraction misregistration (subtraction error) and T1 shine-through.

Subtraction Error (Motion Misregistration)
Subtraction error results from motion between the pre-contrast and post-contrast acquisitions. When the two volumes are not spatially registered, tissue that moved between acquisitions leaves a residual signal difference on the subtracted image that does not represent enhancement. The subtraction artifact appears as a bright crescent or ring adjacent to a structure boundary â€” most commonly at the chest wall, heart margin, or skin surface â€” with a corresponding dark crescent on the opposite side. On more severe misregistration, entire regions of enhancing tissue can be suppressed or false enhancement can be introduced in areas with no underlying pathology. DCE series with high temporal resolution are less susceptible because the shorter acquisition window limits motion between phases. Retrospective rigid or deformable registration algorithms can reduce but not always eliminate this artifact. When subtraction images appear suspicious, reviewing the source (non-subtracted) post-contrast images is essential to determine whether enhancement is real.

T1 Shine-Through
T1 shine-through occurs when a structure with intrinsically short T1 relaxation time â€” and therefore high signal on T1-weighted images at baseline â€” retains high signal on post-contrast images and appears bright on the subtraction image despite the absence of gadolinium uptake. Common causes in breast MRI include hemorrhage or blood products (methemoglobin), proteinaceous cyst fluid, mucinous tissue, fat (when fat suppression is incomplete), and silicone. Because these structures are bright on both pre- and post-contrast images, incomplete cancellation on subtraction can produce a residual high-signal focus that mimics enhancement. Recognition requires comparison of pre-contrast source images: a focus that is already bright before contrast on T1-weighted images and shows no signal increase between phases does not represent true enhancement. T2-weighted imaging and kinetic analysis can help characterize indeterminate findings.

NOISE AND SNR ARTIFACTS: ZIPPER AND HERRINGBONE

Signal-to-noise ratio (SNR) in breast MRI is determined by field strength, voxel volume, acquisition bandwidth, number of signal averages, and coil configuration. Compromised SNR produces grainy, low-contrast images that reduce lesion conspicuity â€” particularly for small enhancing foci. Beyond general SNR degradation, two structured noise artifacts arise from specific technical failures and produce characteristic patterns.

Zipper Artifact
The zipper artifact appears as a discrete linear band of alternating bright and dark pixels, oriented perpendicular to the frequency-encode direction and running across the entire image in the phase-encode direction. It is caused by a single-frequency radiofrequency interference source that continuously adds energy at one frequency during data acquisition. Common sources include leaks in the RF-shielded scanner room (a gap in the Faraday cage, an improperly sealed door, or a cable feedthrough), malfunctioning electronic equipment within the room, or a defective coil element generating internal RF emission. The distinguishing feature is its fixed spatial location â€” the line does not move between acquisitions and is present throughout the series. Diagnosis is confirmed by identifying and eliminating the RF source. Engineering inspection of room shielding and coil integrity is the appropriate response to a persistent zipper artifact that cannot be resolved by repositioning equipment.

Herringbone (Crisscross) Artifact
The herringbone artifact, also called the crisscross artifact, produces a characteristic zigzag or crisscross pattern of bright and dark lines across the entire image. It results from corruption or a spike in k-space data â€” a single anomalously high-amplitude data point in k-space produces a sinusoidal modulation across the entire reconstructed image in both directions, creating the intersecting diagonal lines. Common causes include electronic transients, gradient amplifier instability, and data transmission errors between the receiver and reconstruction system. Unlike the zipper artifact, the herringbone pattern typically affects the entire image in both directions. Automatic k-space spike detection and filtering algorithms can identify and exclude corrupted data points. Repeating the affected sequence often resolves the artifact if the cause was a transient event; persistent herringbone requires engineering evaluation of gradient electronics and reconstruction chain integrity.

PREVENTION AND QUALITY CONTROL

Artifact prevention is most effective when treated as a standardized workflow rather than ad hoc troubleshooting. The goal is to detect fixable quality issues early, before contrast injection and before the patient leaves the scanner.

Pre-Scan Checklist
Patient Preparation and Screening
Review prior breast surgeries, clips, ports, and known sources of artifact before protocol selection
Explain scan duration and need for stillness; perform brief breathing coaching before table-in
Use comfort supports to reduce shoulder, neck, and chest wall strain that can drive motion
Coil Setup and Positioning
Verify symmetric breast placement and full dependent positioning within the coil apertures
Minimize air gaps between breast and coil
Check for skin folds and tissue folding on scouts; reposition before starting dynamic sequences
Confirm phase-encode direction and planned saturation-band placement to limit aliasing
Protocol and System Readiness
Confirm complete anatomic coverage on scouts (posterior tissue, axillary tail, lateral and inferior breast)
Optimize shim-box size and placement before contrast by centering on breast regions of interest to maximize fat-saturation uniformity; minimize non-target lung/air when feasible
Run and review first non-contrast fat-sat sequence for bilateral uniformity before proceeding
If early ghosting is present, correct positioning/coaching issues immediately and reacquire baseline images
Equipment QC
Perform routine breast-coil channel checks and document failed elements
Track recurring scanner-specific issues (fat-sat asymmetry, Nyquist ghosting, RF spikes) in a local log
Schedule periodic B0 calibration and RF shielding checks per vendor QC policy

During-Scan Decision Rules
Repeat any sequence with severe motion or subtraction misregistration before moving to later phases
If fat suppression is non-diagnostic, re-shim first; then switch to Dixon or STIR only when needed
If wrap-around persists despite oversampling, increase FOV or add targeted saturation bands outside FOV
If susceptibility obscures a key region, add a problem-solving sequence (SE or metal-reduction option) only for that target

Post-Scan Quality Sign-Off
Confirm key sequences have adequate quality before patient dismissal
Record repeat sequences and root causes to support protocol refinement and technologist feedback
Use periodic team review of nondiagnostic exams to update local artifact-prevention playbooks

CONCLUSION

Artifacts in breast MRI are pervasive and multifactorial, arising from field inhomogeneity, incomplete fat suppression, metallic hardware, motion, aliasing, chemical shift, geometric distortion, partial volume effects, Gibbs ringing, enhancement misregistration, T1 shine-through, and structured noise patterns. These artifacts alter signal behavior, spatial fidelity, and enhancement conspicuity in ways that can simulate or obscure pathology. Because artifacts change image appearance in predictable, physics-based ways, understanding their origins supports consistent recognition and troubleshooting.
Modern MRI systems and protocol standardization enable substantial artifact mitigation when properly applied. Radiologists and technologists must work collaboratively to recognize artifacts promptly, troubleshoot root causes, and adopt standardized acquisition and quality-assurance workflows. Importantly, no single mitigation strategy is universally applicable â€” effective artifact management requires matching the solution to the underlying cause and accepting that corrective measures often involve trade-offs among SNR, spatial resolution, and scan time.
